  "name": "Question 15",
  "problem": "${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{7{e}^{3x-8}}{3}=$",
  "worked_solution": "We can use the algebra of limits to rewrite the given limit, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{7{e}^{3x-8}}{3} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{7}{3}\\cdot {e}^{3x}\\cdot {e}^{-8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{7{e}^{-8}}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we recall the graph of $y={e}^{3x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13943873/assets/df39137953015b819f3143e823f042ac759a8dfb356821ce6f623b40924b0b0c.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the function decreases to zero as $x$ decreases. Therefore, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{e}^{3x}=0.\n$$\n\nWe conclude that\n\n$$\n\\frac{7{e}^{-8}}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{e}^{3x}=\\frac{7{e}^{-8}}{3}\\cdot 0=0.\n$$",
